{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":83018244,"ns":0,"title":"Alexander's subbase lemma","extract":"In mathematics, specifically topology, Alexander's subbase lemma states that for a topological space to be a compact, it is necessary and sufficient that each open cover of the space consisting of sets in a (fixed) subbase has a finite subcover. Precisely,

given a topological space 



        X


    {\displaystyle X}

 and a subbase 



        S


    {\displaystyle S}

 for it, a subset 



        K
        ⊂
        X


    {\displaystyle K\subset X}

 is compact if and only if for each cover of 



        K


    {\displaystyle K}

 consisting of sets in 



        S


    {\displaystyle S}

, there exists a finite subcover of it.","description":"Theorem in topology","descriptionsource":"local"}]}}
